The motivation is mostly selfish;  to get 'gridSVG' to work with "gtable" objects, we need to replicate what happens when a "gtable" object is drawn (because it does not draw in the standard way).  That effectively means we have to replicate the actions of grid.draw() and drawDetails() and preDrawDetails() methods.  We can do this by inspecting the 'gtable' code, which is nasty, but it would be a bit less nasty if we could do at least some of the replication by calling 'gtable' functions.  Specifically, if there was a function, say gtable.grob(), that grid.draw.gtable() called to create a gTree then we could more easily replicate the actions of grid.draw.gtable() by also calling gtable.grob().  Does that make sense?

This problem does also echo a wider issue, which you have alluded to, which is how to access the components of a grob (or gTree) that only creates its components at drawing time (grid.xaxis() is a good example).  The current solutions are (i) give up (ii) provide access via gEdit() and friends (see the 'edits' argument to grid.xaxis()), but I have wondered about a third way that allows a grob (or gTree) to be "flattened" so that it is actually replaced by its components on the grid display list when it draws.  I haven't thought through a proper design of this idea, but it might involve breaking the current drawDetails() approach into separate build-something-to-draw step followed by a drawing-it step (which is sort of what I am suggesting with the gtable.grob() function request).

Paul

On Wednesday, September 26, 2012 4:07:47 AM UTC+12, Winston Chang wrote:
> Paul - What would be the purpose of returning a gTree? I ask is because gTrees have nice methods for displaying all the children (grid.ls <http://grid.ls>) and retrieving specific child objects (getGrob), but gtables don't. Presently, traversing a gtable hierarchy is complicated. This is a part of gtable that could use improvement and it would be nice to get some input on how best to do that.
>
> -Winston
>
>
> On Mon, Sep 24, 2012 at 11:02 PM, Paul Murrell <pmu...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > On Tuesday, September 25, 2012 1:19:39 PM UTC+12, kohske wrote:
> > > Hi,
> > >
> > > > Still think that having grid.draw.gtable() and grid.draw.gtableChild() but preDrawDetails.gtableParent() is not terribly nice (even if it's my fault for the 'grid' design that you are having to work around :).
> > >
> > > Yes, I agree. I don't like my implementation but this is the easiest way I could find...
> > >
> > > > One option might be to enforce a 'vp' of NULL for "gtableChild" objects (via a validDetails() method);
> > > But a child of gtable may be gtable, and vp cannot be NULL in this case
> >
> > Ok.  I see that now.  Thanks again for the clarification.
> >
> > > > alternatively, could you use a grid.draw.gtableParent() method (even though it is not strictly necessary) just to make things a bit more consistent ?
> > >
> > > Probably.
> >
> > I understand if this does not rank very high on the todo list :)  Just a suggestion in case it helps.
> >
> > > Or, can drawGTree have a kind of hook function before calling pushvpgp(x), say, prePushVpgp(x)
> >
> > I've pretty much regretted the "hook" approach from the very beginning, so another hook function is unlikely.  I prefer your solution of writing a method for the higher-level function (grid.draw() in this case).
> >
> > Thanks again for your replies.
> >
> > Paul
